  "query_preview": "..\n\nCold troughing will fade today as one final lobe of 500mb absolute \nvorticity pivots out of Southeast Michigan this morning and 1000-\n500mb geopotential heights rise. Both the plan view perspective of \nmoisture on isentropic surfaces and forecast soundings support \nstratus/stratocumulus staying in place over the forecast area for \nthe daylight hours today with saturation at the base of the midlevel \nsubsidence inversion. Classic representation of stratocumulus \ndownwind of the lake aggregate here in Lower Michigan. The cloud \nwill limit highs today in the middle 40s, some 5 degrees or so below \nnormal.\n\nMidlevel shortwave ridge feature is forecasted to build through the \nstate tonight before a much larger omega block then expands in \nwavelength through eastern North America for much of the week. \nCenter of mid to upper level high pressure tracks through Southeast \nMichigan tonight setting the stage for a long duration of southwest \nflow and subsequent warming. Forecast soundings support dry weather \nthis week with a sub saturated air mass in the lowest 20.0 kft agl. \nHigh temperatures this week are expected to top out in the middle \n50s by Tuesday (5-8 degrees above normal) and lower 60s by Friday \nand Saturday (10-15 degrees above normal). Southwest winds will \nremain seasonally modest throughout much of the week with gradient \npossibly supporting 20 to 30 mph by the end of the week.",
